% ============================================================================
% figurative-language-type — three named figures of speech and what each
% actually does (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Writers reach for different figures of speech to do different jobs: a
% metaphor makes an implicit comparison, personification hands human traits
% to something that isn't human, hyperbole exaggerates for effect. Recall
% is a binding query:
%
%     ? figurative_language_type(metaphor, $D)
%       % describes_something_in_a_way_thats_not_literally_true_to_make_a_comparison
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `figurative_language_type(type, description)` carrying the
% source citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query
% — the engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with
% zero model calls, and abstains on a figurative-language term that is
% not one of these three. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a
% description and recall the type it names:
%
%     ? figurative_language_type($T, gives_human_characteristics_to_nonhuman_or_abstract_things)
%       % personification
%
% The three figures of speech, as a little truth table:
%
%     type              description                                                                  quote (verbatim)
%     ----------------  -------------------------------------------------------------------------    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     metaphor           describes_something_in_a_way_thats_not_literally_true_to_make_a_comparison    "A metaphor describes an object or action in a way that isn't literally true but helps explain an idea or make a comparison."
%     personification     gives_human_characteristics_to_nonhuman_or_abstract_things                   "Personification means giving human characteristics to nonhuman or abstract things."
%     hyperbole           a_great_exaggeration_used_to_add_emphasis                                    "Hyperbole is a great exaggeration, often unrealistic, to add emphasis to a sentiment."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "Figurative Language
% Examples: 6 Common Types and Definitions" article, which introduces each
% of these under its own "Definition:"-labeled sentence. WebFetch-verified
% before writing (twice — the second pass specifically confirmed each
% sentence's exact wording and that it stands alone as a complete
% definition before any follow-up example sentence). `trust consensus` —
% Grammarly is a reputable secondary reference, the same tier the sibling
% `noun-type.adj`/`verb-type.adj`/`pronoun-type.adj`/`preposition-type.adj`/
% `conjunction-type.adj`/`determiner-type.adj`/`end-punctuation-mark.adj`
% (the other libraries in this directory) already use for their Grammarly
% citations. A figure of speech that is NOT one of these three —
% "allusion", a real device the same page also names with its own clean
% defining sentence, but one that works by referencing an external work,
% person, or event rather than by comparison, exaggeration, or
% personification — a different rhetorical mechanism than the three
% tabled here — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it
% rather than inventing a description. Simile and idiom, also named on
% the same page, are deliberately excluded too: both are already
% grounded as their own separately-shipped libraries in this directory
% (`simile-meaning.adj`, `idiom-meaning.adj`), so tabling them again here
% under a different predicate would duplicate coverage rather than add
% to it. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table figurative_language_type {
    columns type, description

    row (metaphor, describes_something_in_a_way_thats_not_literally_true_to_make_a_comparison)
    row (personification, gives_human_characteristics_to_nonhuman_or_abstract_things)
    row (hyperbole, a_great_exaggeration_used_to_add_emphasis)

    source "A metaphor describes an object or action in a way that isn't literally true but helps explain an idea or make a comparison."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/writing-tips/figurative-language/"
    trust consensus
}
